<label>*</label>Corresponding authors. Address: Department of Radiology, Guangdong Provincial People&#x02019;s Hospital (Guangdong Academy of Medical Sciences), Southern Medical University, Guangzhou, People&#x02019;s Republic of China. Tel.: +86 020 838 278 12; E-mail: <email>liuzaiyi@gdph.org.cn</email> (Z. Liu); Tel.: +86 020 838 278 12. E-mail: <email>liangchanghong@gdph.org.cn</email> (C. Liang), and Department of Radiology, Zhujiang Hospital, Southern Medical University, Guangzhou, People's Republic of China. Tel.: +86 020 616 434 60. E-mail: <email>quanxianyue2014@163.com</email> (X. Quan).</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>5</month><year>2024</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>21</day><month>2</month><year>2024</year></pub-date><volume>110</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>2556</fpage><lpage>2567</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>5</day><month>12</month><year>2023</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>31</day><month>1</month><year>2024</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2024 The Author(s). Published by Wolters Kluwer Health, Inc.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2024</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-No Derivatives License 4.0</ext-link> (CCBY-NC-ND), where it is permissible to download and share the work provided it is properly cited. The work cannot be changed in any way or used commercially without permission from the journal. <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link>
</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xlink:href="js9-110-2556.pdf"/><abstract><sec><title>Background:</title><p>Although postoperative adjuvant transarterial chemoembolization (PA-TACE) improves survival outcomes in a subset of patients with resected hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), the lack of reliable biomarkers for patient selection remains a significant challenge. The present study aimed to evaluate whether computed tomography imaging can provide more value for predicting benefits from PA-TACE and to establish a new scheme for guiding PA-TACE benefits.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods:</title><p>In this retrospective study, patients with HCC who had undergone preoperative contrast-enhanced computed tomography and curative hepatectomy were evaluated. Inverse probability of treatment weight was performed to balance the difference of baseline characteristics. Cox models were used to test the interaction among PA-TACE, imaging features, and pathological indicators. An HCC imaging and pathological classification (HIPC) scheme incorporating these imaging and pathological indicators was established.</p></sec><sec><title>Results:</title><p>This study included 1488 patients [median age, 52 years (IQR, 45&#x02013;61 years); 1309 male]. Microvascular invasion (MVI) positive, and diameter &#x0003e;5&#x000a0;cm tumors achieved a higher recurrence-free survival (RFS), and overall survival (OS) benefit, respectively, from PA-TACE than MVI negative, and diameter &#x02264;5&#x000a0;cm tumors. Patients with internal arteries (IA) positive benefited more than those with IA-negative in terms of RFS (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>=0.016) and OS (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>=0.018). PA-TACE achieved significant RFS and OS improvements in HIPC3 (IA present and diameter &#x0003e;5 cm, or two or three tumors) patients but not in HIPC1 (diameter &#x02264;5&#x000a0;cm, MVI negative) and HIPC2 (other single tumor) patients. Our scheme may decrease the number of patients receiving PA-TACE by ~36.5% compared to the previous suggestion.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusions:</title><p>IA can provide more value for predicting the benefit of PA-TACE treatment. The proposed HIPC scheme can be used to stratify patients with and without survival benefits from PA-TACE.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group><title>Keywords:</title><kwd>contrast-enhanced computed tomography</kwd><kwd>hepatectomy</kwd><kwd>hepatocellular carcinoma</kwd><kwd>prognosis</kwd><kwd>transarterial chemoembolization</kwd></kwd-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>OPEN-ACCESS</meta-name><meta-value>TRUE</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>SDC</meta-name><meta-value>T</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="intro"><title>Introduction</title><boxed-text content-type="shaded" position="anchor"><caption><p>Highlights</p></caption><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Microvascular invasion and internal arteries reliably served as predictive imaging biomarkers of benefit from postoperative adjuvant transarterial chemoembolization (PA-TACE) in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>The proposed hepatocellular carcinoma imaging and pathological classification scheme comprehensively integrated tumor diameter, tumor number, microvascular invasion, and internal arteries, and can stratify patients with and without survival benefit from PA-TACE.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Approximately 36.5% patients may relieve of undergoing PA-TACE by following our scheme compared to the previous recommendation.</p></list-item></list></boxed-text><p>Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is the sixth most commonly diagnosed cancer, and the fourth leading cause of cancer-related mortality death<sup><xref rid="R1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref></sup>. Hepatectomy is the first-line treatment for HCC, especially early-stage HCC, which has a better prognosis after resection<sup><xref rid="R2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>,<xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref></sup>. The 5-year recurrence rate after hepatectomy is 50&#x02013;75%<sup><xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref></sup>. Postoperative adjuvant transarterial chemoembolization (PA-TACE) therapy has been demonstrated effective in prolonging long-term survival outcomes and reducing tumor recurrence rates in HCC patients<sup><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref></sup>. However, some studies have shown a lack of efficacy. Therefore, careful consideration of patient selection for PA-TACE is crucial to avoid worse outcomes, increased toxicities, and interventional procedure risks<sup><xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref></sup>. Therefore, there is an urgent need to identify reliable markers for guiding HCC PA-TACE.</p><p>PA-TACE prolongs survival outcomes in high-risk patients, such as those with microvascular invasion (MVI), tumor size &#x0003e;5&#x000a0;cm, and multinodular disease<sup><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref></sup>. However, no uniform standards for PA-TACE exist<sup><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="R6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref></sup>, which may lead to high-risk patients not being treated effectively and low-risk patients being overtreated. The expression of Cochlin (COCH), RPB5-mediating protein (RMP), Cezanne, and circulating tumor cells may predict PA-TACE benefit<sup><xref rid="R13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref></sup>. However, these methods require additional immunohistochemistry or detection of circulating tumor cells, increasing the financial burden for patients and the workload for surgeons.</p><p>Dynamic contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CT) or MRI are the preferred imaging examination methods for HCC definitive diagnosis, and are routinely performed preoperatively<sup><xref rid="R17" ref-type="bibr">17</xref></sup>. Imaging features, such as internal arteries (IA), two-trait predictor of venous invasion (TTPVI), and incomplete capsule, can preoperatively and noninvasively predict MVI or survival outcomes<sup><xref rid="R18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref></sup>. Therefore, we hypothesized that imaging may help identify patients who will benefit from PA-TACE. However, the current research on this topic is limited.</p><p>The present study explored whether CT imaging could provide more value for predicting PA-TACE benefits and aimed to establish a new scheme for guiding PA-TACE treatment based on CT imaging features.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods"><title>Methods</title><sec sec-type="subjects"><title>Patients</title><p>The surgical and histological databases of two medical institutions were searched to identify patients who underwent hepatectomy for HCC, between January 2012 and December 2017 in the Sun Yat-sen University Cancer Center, and between January 2012 and December 2020 in the Zhujiang Hospital of Southern Medical University. The study was approved by the Institutional Ethics Review Board of the two medical centers, and the requirement for written informed consent was waived. In addition, this study was registered at ClinicalTrials.gov (trail number: NCT06156748). The study inclusion criteria were: (a) CT scans acquired within 1 month prior to the surgery; (b) confirmation of HCC diagnosis through pathological examination; and (c) curative surgical resection. The exclusion criteria were: (a) prior antitumor treatment or other adjuvant therapies such as targeted therapy, hepatic arterial infusion chemotherapy, or cytokine-induced killer cell immunotherapy; (b) presence of macrovascular thrombosis or metastasis; (c) perioperative mortality; (d) presence of image artifacts; and (e) tumor rupture; and (f) unreported MVI status. Additional details on the preoperative clinical and postoperative pathological data, follow-up protocol and CT data acquisition are described in Supplementary Methods and Table S1 (Supplemental Digital Content 1, <ext-link xlink:href="http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998" ext-link-type="uri">http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998</ext-link>). This retrospective study has been reported in line with the strengthening the reporting of cohort, cross-sectional, and case&#x02013;control studies in surgery (STROCSS) criteria<sup><xref rid="R23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref></sup> (Supplemental Digital Content 2, <ext-link xlink:href="http://links.lww.com/JS9/B999" ext-link-type="uri">http://links.lww.com/JS9/B999</ext-link>).</p></sec><sec><title>PA-TACE</title><p>PA-TACE was performed 1&#x02013;2 months after hepatectomy. Using a catheter inserted through the femoral artery to access the hepatic artery, a microcatheter was employed on both the right and left hepatic arteries, administering adriamycin (20&#x02013;30&#x000a0;mg/m<sup>2</sup>) and lipiodol (3&#x02013;5&#x000a0;ml).</p></sec><sec><title>Image analysis</title><p>Imaging features for each HCC were evaluated, including: tumor diameter; number of tumors (single vs. two or three tumors)<sup><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup>; IA, denoting the presence of discrete arteries within the tumor<sup><xref rid="R24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>,<xref rid="R25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref></sup>; hypoattenuating halos; intratumoral necrosis<sup><xref rid="R24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref></sup>; radiologic capsule appearance<sup><xref rid="R24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref></sup>; margin<sup><xref rid="R24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>,<xref rid="R26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref></sup>; peritumoral enhancement<sup><xref rid="R26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref></sup>; and TTPVI<sup><xref rid="R24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>,<xref rid="R26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref></sup>. Details of image analysis, definitions and examples of these imaging features are shown in Supplementary Methods Table S2 (Supplemental Digital Content 1, <ext-link xlink:href="http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998" ext-link-type="uri">http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998</ext-link>) and Fig. S1 (Supplemental Digital Content 1, <ext-link xlink:href="http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998" ext-link-type="uri">http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998</ext-link>).</p></sec><sec><title>Risk groups</title><p>Patients with: tumor diameter &#x0003e;5&#x000a0;cm and MVI negative were in the intermediate risk group, single tumor with MVI positive or 2&#x02013;3 tumors were in the high-risk group; the rest were in the low-risk group<sup><xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref></sup>.</p></sec><sec><title>Statistical analysis</title><p>The interobserver agreement between the two radiologists assessing the CT imaging features was measured and is presented in Table S3 (Supplemental Digital Content 1, <ext-link xlink:href="http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998" ext-link-type="uri">http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998</ext-link>). Hazard ratios (HRs), 95% CIs, and <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values for the interactions between clinical data, imaging features, pathological indicators, and PA-TACE benefit associated with recurrence-free survival (RFS) and overall survival (OS) were analyzed using inverse probability of treatment weighting-adjusted Cox proportional hazard regression analysis<sup><xref rid="R27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref></sup>. Interaction <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.10 was considered significant<sup><xref rid="R28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>,<xref rid="R29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref></sup>. The &#x02018;forestplot&#x02019; package was used to perform forest plots<sup><xref rid="R30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref></sup>. The Sankey diagram was generated online (<ext-link xlink:href="https://dycharts.com/appv2/#/pages/home/index" ext-link-type="uri">https://dycharts.com/appv2/#/pages/home/index</ext-link>)<sup><xref rid="R31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref></sup>. Statistical significance was set at <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.05 for a two-sided test.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results"><title>Results</title><sec sec-type="subjects"><title>Patient characteristics</title><p>A total 1488 patients (1309 male) with median age of 52 years (IQR, 45&#x02013;61 years) were included (Fig. S2, Supplemental Digital Content 1, <ext-link xlink:href="http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998" ext-link-type="uri">http://links.lww.com/JS9/B998</ext-link>), 414 (27.8%) of whom received PA-TACE. Baseline data for the entire cohort, stratified by postoperative adjuvant treatment are described in Table <xref rid="T1" ref-type="table">1</xref>. There were no significant differences in the distribution of clinicopathological characteristics between the groups, except the Edmondson&#x02013;Steiner grade and albumin (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.001). After the inverse probability of treatment weighting adjustment, all standardized differences were lower than 0.1, indicating negligible baseline differences. The median follow-up duration was 60 months (IQR, 28&#x02013;88 months). In total, 517 patients died during follow-up (median, 28 months; IQR, 15&#x02013;49 months), and 805 patients experienced a recurrence (median, 11 months; IQR, 5&#x02013;29 months). In the primary analysis of the overall cohort, PA-TACE did not significantly affect RFS or OS (Fig. <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>A, B). After the inverse probability of treatment weighting, the PA-TACE group had a higher RFS rate, while the OS rate was similar (Fig. <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>C, D).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Demographic data and baseline clinical characteristics of the study patients.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="3" axis="1">Individuals, No. (%) (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=1488)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="2" axis="1">Standardized difference</th></tr><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Variable</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Total (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=1488)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Hepatectomy Alone (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=1074)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">PA-TACE (<italic toggle="yes">n</italic>=414)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Unweighted<xref rid="T1fn1" ref-type="table-fn"><sup>a</sup></xref>
</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Weighted<xref rid="T1fn2" ref-type="table-fn"><sup>b</sup></xref>
</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age (years, IQR)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">52 (45&#x02013;61)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">53 (45&#x02013;62)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">51 (43&#x02013;61)</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.12</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.03</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="6">Sex</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Female</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">179 (12.0%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">131 (12.2%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">48 (11.6%)</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.02</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Male</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1309 (88.0%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">943 (87.8%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">366 (88.4%)</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.02</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="6">MVI</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Negative</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">940 (63.2%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">691 (64.3%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">251 (60.6%)</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.08</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.04</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Positive</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">548 (36.8%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">383 (35.7%)</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">163 (39.4%)</td><td align="char" char="." ro